  "body": "Spinning off from https://github.com/whatwg/html/pull/4214#discussion_r253237512.\r\n\r\nhttps://html.spec.whatwg.org/#event-loop-processing-model has a lot of usages of \"If there are browsing contexts\" or \"for each browsing context\". It's unclear what the scope of these are, and in general it seems non-obvious how to navigate from the given inputs to the algorithm (roughly, an event loop and a task) to the browsing contexts in question.\r\n\r\nHere's my audit. All step numbers refer to post-#4214.\r\n\r\n- Step 2.2: goes from event loop -> \"all Document objects associated\" -> top-level BC\r\n  - Is the association between Documents and event loops well-defined?\r\n- Step 9: task -> script evaluation environment settings object set -> top-level BC\r\n  - Solid.\r\n- Step 10.1: event loop -> \"all Document objects associated\"\r\n  - Same as 2.2, is this well-defined?\r\n- Step 10.2, 10.3, 10.4: \"if there are browsing contexts\"\r\n  - This seems like it should be rephrased by iterating for each _doc_ in _docs_, and getting _doc_'s associated BC, and then if the condition on the BC is not met, removing _doc_ from _docs_.\r\n  - So, not broken, just could be clearer.\r\n- Step 11: \"none of the browsing contexts\", \"for each browsing context\"\r\n  - This seems pretty sketchy.\r\n  - Our real target here is Window objects. Perhaps we should be going event loop -> all associated Window objects -> Window's document's BC, or similar?\r\n- Step 12: _docs_ from step 10.1 -> top-level BCs of those _docs_\r\n  - Solid.\r\n\r\nSo, not as bad as I feared, with only one actually-unclear step (11), plus the bigger question of whether event loop -> \"all Document objects associated\" is a valid chain.",
